      a: "Yes — and you are not changing who you are, you are adjusting a single channel of communication for a specific context. Think of it like adjusting your code formatting for a team's style guide — you are not becoming a different developer, you are adapting to increase clarity. The technique: practise prosodic emphasis. Choose a key statement — 'I reduced the regression suite execution time from forty-five minutes to twelve minutes.' Record yourself saying it flat. Then record yourself saying it with emphasis — slightly slower and slightly louder on 'forty-five minutes' and 'twelve minutes.' Listen to both. The difference is dramatic, and the emphasised version takes no more effort — it just requires awareness. Practise with five technical statements you are likely to use in the interview. Then practise your full answers with natural emphasis. Within two to three practice sessions, prosodic emphasis will start to feel natural — because it is natural. Humans use vocal variation instinctively when they are relaxed and engaged. You are not learning a new skill. You are removing the interview anxiety that suppresses the skill you already have. Mitchell's <a href='/blog/qa-imposter-syndrome-sdet-confidence-interview-tips-2026'>QA imposter syndrome guide</a> explores the confidence-communication connection in detail."
