    {
      q: "How do I run Selenium tests on GitLab CI with multiple browsers in parallel?",
      a: "GitLab CI's <code>parallel:matrix</code> and <code>services:</code> keywords make cross-browser Selenium testing straightforward. The pattern: define one job with a matrix of browser/hub combinations, and use <code>services:</code> to start Selenium standalone containers for each browser. Example: <code>selenium-tests: stage: test image: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 services: - name: selenium/standalone-chrome:latest alias: chrome - name: selenium/standalone-firefox:latest alias: firefox - name: selenium/standalone-edge:latest alias: edge parallel: matrix: - BROWSER: chrome HUB_HOST: chrome - BROWSER: firefox HUB_HOST: firefox - BROWSER: edge HUB_HOST: edge script: - mvn test -Dbrowser=\$BROWSER -Dgrid.url=http://\$HUB_HOST:4444/wd/hub artifacts: reports: junit: target/surefire-reports/TEST-*.xml when: always</code>. This creates three parallel jobs — Chrome, Firefox, Edge — each connecting to its own Selenium standalone container via the alias hostname. The Selenium containers start alongside the job and are destroyed when the job completes — zero persistent infrastructure, zero Selenium Grid maintenance. For larger test suites, combine with Playwright-style sharding: <code>parallel: matrix: - BROWSER: [chrome, firefox, edge] SHARD: [1, 2, 3, 4]</code> creates 12 parallel jobs — each browser with 4 shards.",
    },
    {
      q: "What is <code>parallel:matrix</code> in GitLab CI and how is it different from Jenkins <code>parallel</code>?",
      a: "GitLab CI's <code>parallel:matrix</code> (introduced in GitLab 13.3) creates a Cartesian product of variable combinations — <code>parallel: matrix: - BROWSER: [chromium, firefox, webkit] OS: [linux, windows]</code> generates 6 parallel jobs, one per browser-OS combination. Each job receives its own <code>BROWSER</code> and <code>OS</code> environment variables. Jenkins <code>parallel</code> is a Groovy-based block that runs explicitly named stages in parallel — <code>parallel { stage('Chrome-Linux') { ... } stage('Firefox-Windows') { ... } }</code>. Key differences: (1) <strong>Declarative vs imperative:</strong> GitLab CI's matrix is declarative — define the dimensions, the framework generates the jobs. Jenkins is imperative — define each stage explicitly. (2) <strong>Scaling:</strong> adding a third browser to GitLab CI requires adding one array element. Adding a third browser to Jenkins requires copying a stage definition and changing the name. (3) <strong>Job naming:</strong> GitLab CI generates job names automatically (<code>selenium-tests: [chrome, linux]</code>). Jenkins requires manual stage names. (4) <strong>Flexibility:</strong> Jenkins <code>parallel</code> enables per-browser customisation (different setup steps per browser) that GitLab CI's matrix (same script for all variants) struggles with. For SDETs: use GitLab CI <code>parallel:matrix</code> when the test logic is identical across browser/OS variants. Use Jenkins <code>parallel</code> (or GitLab CI with separate jobs per browser) when per-browser customisation is needed.",
    },
    {
      q: "How does <code>needs</code> work in GitLab CI, and when should I use it for test automation pipelines?",
      a: "The <code>needs</code> keyword creates a directed acyclic graph (DAG) pipeline by declaring explicit job dependencies — <code>needs: [\"build-playwright-image\"]</code>. Without <code>needs</code>, GitLab CI pipelines execute stages sequentially — all jobs in the 'build' stage must complete before any job in the 'test' stage starts. With <code>needs</code>, a test job starts as soon as its declared dependency completes, even if other build-stage jobs are still running. Use <code>needs</code> for test automation when: (1) your pipeline has multiple test frameworks (Playwright, Selenium, Cypress) and each framework's test jobs depend on a specific build job, not the entire build stage; (2) the pipeline duration is dominated by stage synchronisation waiting — all build jobs complete in 5 minutes, but one slow build job takes 12 minutes, and every test job waits for it; (3) your report job needs artifacts from specific test jobs, not all jobs in the test stage — <code>needs: - job: test-playwright artifacts: true</code> downloads artifacts from a specific job. Don't use <code>needs</code> when: (1) the pipeline is simple (fewer than 10 jobs) — the DAG complexity is not worth the marginal speed improvement; (2) pipeline clarity matters more than speed — DAG pipelines are harder to visualise; (3) stage boundaries provide useful failure isolation. At HMRC, Mitchell's team used <code>needs</code> to reduce the tax-calculation pipeline from 42 to 18 minutes — each test group started as soon as its build job completed, bypassing the stage boundary.",
    },
    {
      q: "What are GitLab CI artifacts and how do I use them for test reporting?",
      a: "GitLab CI artifacts are files that a job produces and passes to subsequent jobs or makes available for download. They are configured with the <code>artifacts:</code> keyword in a job definition. For test reporting: (1) <strong>JUnit test reports:</strong> <code>artifacts: reports: junit: test-results/junit.xml</code> — publishes JUnit XML to GitLab's merge request test report. The merge request's 'Tests' tab displays test names, statuses, durations, and error messages. (2) <strong>Downloadable artifacts:</strong> <code>artifacts: paths: - playwright-report/ - test-results/ when: always expire_in: 7 days</code> — archives the Playwright HTML report and test results as downloadable pipeline artifacts. <code>when: always</code> ensures artifacts are published even when tests fail (critical for debugging). <code>expire_in</code> prevents infinite artifact accumulation. (3) <strong>Coverage reports:</strong> <code>artifacts: reports: coverage_report: coverage_format: cobertura path: coverage/cobertura-coverage.xml</code> — publishes code coverage data displayed in the merge request diff. (4) <strong>Dotenv reports:</strong> <code>artifacts: reports: dotenv: test-variables.env</code> — passes environment variables from test jobs to downstream jobs. Key distinction: <code>artifacts</code> are for intra-pipeline data flow (passing data between jobs); <code>cache</code> is for inter-pipeline acceleration (caching dependencies across pipeline runs). Confusing the two — using artifacts for dependency caching — creates artifact bloat and slows down pipelines.",
    },
    {
      q: "How do I configure a Docker executor for test automation in GitLab CI?",
      a: "The Docker executor is the recommended executor for test automation because each job runs in an isolated container with a specified image — zero accumulated state, reproducible environments, and no 'works on my machine' flakiness. Configuration: (1) install GitLab Runner with the Docker executor: <code>gitlab-runner register --executor docker --docker-image alpine:latest</code>; (2) specify the Docker image in <code>.gitlab-ci.yml</code>: <code>default: image: mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.44.0-jammy</code> (global default) or <code>playwright-tests: image: mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.44.0-jammy</code> (per-job); (3) configure the runner's <code>config.toml</code>: <code>[[runners]] executor = \"docker\" [runners.docker] image = \"alpine:latest\" privileged = false shm_size = 0</code>. Key settings: <code>privileged = false</code> (security — don't run test containers as root unless absolutely necessary for Docker-in-Docker), <code>shm_size = 0</code> (use host's /dev/shm for Chrome's shared memory — prevents Chrome crashes on memory-intensive test suites; <code>0</code> means 'use default, which is usually 64MB' — increase to <code>256m</code> or <code>512m</code> for large test suites), <code>volumes = [\"/cache\"]</code> (persistent volume for GitLab CI cache — primarily stores <code>node_modules</code>, Playwright browser binaries, and Maven/Gradle dependencies across pipeline runs). For the Playwright image specifically, pin the version (<code>v1.44.0-jammy</code>) — never use <code>latest</code>, which causes browser version changes without notice and breaks tests.",
    },
    {
      q: "What is Auto DevOps in GitLab, and how does it affect test automation?",
      a: "Auto DevOps is GitLab's zero-configuration CI/CD pipeline — when enabled at the project level, GitLab automatically detects the project's language and framework, generates a pipeline with Build, Test, Code Quality, SAST, Container Scanning, License Compliance, Review App, DAST, and Production stages, and deploys to Kubernetes. For SDETs, Auto DevOps is significant because it includes a 'Test' stage by default — and the SDET's role is to customise that stage for framework-specific test execution. Customise by extending Auto DevOps in <code>.gitlab-ci.yml</code>: <code>include: - template: Auto-DevOps.gitlab-ci.yml test: image: mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.44.0-jammy script: - npm ci - npx playwright test artifacts: reports: junit: test-results/junit.xml</code>. This overrides the default Auto DevOps 'test' job with a Playwright-specific job while inheriting all other Auto DevOps stages (Build, Code Quality, SAST, etc.). For Selenium: <code>test: image: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 services: - selenium/standalone-chrome:latest script: - mvn test artifacts: reports: junit: target/surefire-reports/TEST-*.xml</code>. The interview signal: describing Auto DevOps not as a replacement for custom pipelines but as a starting point — it provides the pipeline scaffold (Build, Test, Deploy stages with standard security scanning), and the SDET customises the Test stage with framework-specific execution, JUnit reporting, and merge request integration. The result: the organisation gets standardised security scanning and deployment (from Auto DevOps) plus a customised test stage (from the SDET's override) — without building the entire pipeline from scratch.",
    }
