    {
      q: "When should I use self-hosted runners instead of GitHub-hosted runners for test automation?",
      a: "Use GitHub-hosted runners (the default) when: you need zero infrastructure maintenance, your test suites run under 6 hours, and your tests fit within the 14GB disk space limit. Use self-hosted runners when: you need specific hardware (GPU for visual testing, 32GB+ RAM for performance testing), you exceed GitHub's concurrency limits (self-hosted runners have no cap), you need more than 14GB disk space (test suites with video recording or large log files), or you need access to internal network resources (databases, APIs) not publicly exposed. At BT, Mitchell's team used self-hosted runners for the broadband provisioning performance suite — it required 32GB RAM, and GitHub-hosted runners couldn't provide that specification. For public repositories, use ephemeral self-hosted runners (--ephemeral flag) that clean up after each job to prevent data leakage between workflow runs."
    },
    {
      q: "How does GitHub Actions matrix strategy work for cross-browser testing?",
      a: "A matrix strategy generates multiple parallel job instances from a single job definition, each with different variable values. For cross-browser testing: define browser, operating system, and shard dimensions in the matrix. Each job receives its combination via environment variables and executes the test suite with that configuration. Use exclude to remove invalid combinations (Safari on Windows, Firefox on iOS) and include to add specific combinations not in the Cartesian product (Chrome Beta). Use max-parallel to limit concurrent jobs and avoid overwhelming external services. Use fail-fast: false when you need results from all browsers even if one fails. The key interview point: explain that matrix jobs are independent (no shared state), require unique artifact names to avoid upload conflicts, and should use conditional artifact upload (if: failure()) to avoid storing passing test data."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I handle Playwright test reporting and debugging in GitHub Actions?",
      a: "Configure Playwright's HTML reporter and trace-on-failure in playwright.config.ts: reporter: [['html', { open: 'never' }]], and trace: 'on-first-retry' (records a trace only when a test fails on the first retry — avoiding performance cost on passing tests). In the workflow: after test execution, upload the HTML report as an artifact using actions/upload-artifact@v4 with if: failure() (only upload on failure to conserve storage). For matrix builds, create a separate reporting job (needs: test) that downloads all browser-specific reports and runs npx playwright merge-reports to produce a combined report. Use Playwright's --reporter=github option to annotate pull requests with failure details directly in the PR interface. For trace debugging: download the trace artifact from the workflow run summary, open with npx playwright show-trace trace.zip, and step through the test execution frame by frame. At the Co-op, this trace-on-failure approach reduced CI failure investigation time by 70%."
    },
    {
      q: "How long are GitHub Actions artifacts retained and how should I manage retention?",
      a: "Artifacts are retained for 90 days by default on all GitHub plans (configurable down to 1 day or up to 400 days on GitHub Enterprise). For CI test runs, set retention to 7 days — long enough to investigate failures, short enough to manage storage costs. For release verification runs, extend to 90 days for compliance audit trails. Use the retention-days parameter on actions/upload-artifact@v4: retention-days: 7. Upload artifacts conditionally (if: failure()) to avoid storing gigabytes of passing test data. Use descriptive artifact names that include run ID, browser, and shard: playwright-report-\u0024{{ github.run_id }}-\u0024{{ matrix.browser }}. Artifacts consume GitHub Actions storage (included in your plan, with additional storage billed beyond the included quota), so conditional upload and short retention are important cost management strategies at scale."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I debug a GitHub Actions workflow that is failing?",
      a: "Start with the workflow run logs in the GitHub Actions tab — each step's output is available, and failed steps show the exit code and error message. For test-specific failures: check the Playwright trace/screenshot/screen recording artifacts (uploaded with if: failure()), check the test report artifact for failure details, and look at the step logs for environment issues (missing dependencies, incorrect Node version, missing environment variables). For workflow-level failures (the workflow doesn't start): check the workflow YAML syntax (GitHub validates YAML on push but subtle errors like incorrect indentation can break trigger matching), verify the trigger conditions (is the workflow configured for the right branches and events?), and check repository/branch protection rules (required checks that are not defined). For runner issues: check the runner's disk space (df -h as a step), memory usage (free -m), and installed software versions (node --version, npm --version). Add a debugging step (run: env | sort) to see all environment variables available to the workflow. For intermittent failures: check if the issue is concurrency-related (multiple matrix jobs competing for the same resource), timing-related (race conditions in test data setup), or environment-related (external service unavailability during the test window)."
    }
