      a: "You have three approaches, which you should present in order of increasing sophistication. First, design the stub from the API specification: if the API contract is defined in an OpenAPI/Swagger spec or a README, use that to create WireMock stub mappings that return the documented response shapes and status codes. Second, use WireMock's recording feature against a prototype or mock implementation: if the API team has a stub server or a partially implemented endpoint, record real traffic against it and replay in your tests. Third, use contract-first development: define a Pact consumer contract that specifies exactly what requests your service will make and what responses it expects. This contract becomes the specification the API team builds against — and your WireMock stubs are generated from the contract, guaranteeing they match what the real API will eventually provide. The key principle: don't guess what the API will return. Derive your stubs from a specification, a prototype, or a contract — something that creates a shared understanding between your team and the API team."
