      q: "How do canary deployments work with feature flags — and how do you test the interaction?",
      a: "Canary deployments and feature flags address different layers of progressive delivery: canary deployments control which version of the code is running (infrastructure layer), while feature flags control which code paths within that version are active (application layer). They can be used independently or together. When used together — deploying a new version via canary while also using feature flags to control feature activation — the testing strategy must cover: (1) both versions work correctly with all flag combinations they support (if the canary version introduces a new feature behind a flag, test the canary with the flag on and the canary with the flag off), (2) the flag state doesn't interact unexpectedly with the canary traffic split (a user might hit the canary with flag-on, then the baseline with flag-off on their next request — unless sticky sessions are configured), and (3) flag toggling during a canary rollout doesn't trigger false-positive canary analysis failures (toggling a flag changes behaviour, which changes metrics, which could trigger a canary analysis failure unrelated to the code deployment). The solution: run a 'flag stability' period before the canary analysis begins — ensure all flags are in their target state and metrics have stabilised before comparing canary to baseline. At Accenture, we enforced a 5-minute flag stabilisation period before any canary analysis window started."
