      q: "What's the most common mistake you see in canary deployment testing strategies?",
      a: "The most common mistake is treating the canary like a staging environment — running the full regression suite against the canary during the rollout instead of using targeted smoke tests and canary analysis. This has three consequences: (1) it delays the rollout (a 200-test regression suite takes 30–60 minutes, during which the canary is stuck at 5% traffic), (2) it contaminates the canary-vs-baseline metrics comparison (regression tests generate artificial traffic that doesn't represent real user behaviour — higher throughput, different endpoint distribution, no think time between requests), and (3) it treats production infrastructure as a test environment (production databases, caches, and message queues are not designed for synthetic load testing alongside real traffic). The correct approach is: comprehensive regression testing in staging (pre-canary), targeted smoke tests (3–5 critical user journeys) against the canary, and canary analysis (real traffic metrics comparison) as the primary regression detection mechanism."
