      a: "A quality gate is an <strong>automated decision point</strong> in your CI/CD pipeline that evaluates build outputs — test results, code coverage, static analysis findings, security scan results, performance benchmarks — against predefined thresholds and produces a binary outcome: proceed or block. The key distinction from a regular pipeline stage is that a stage <em>runs tasks</em> (execute tests, run linter, scan dependencies) while a quality gate <em>evaluates outcomes</em> (are the test results good enough? does the coverage meet the threshold?). For example, a test stage runs 500 tests. A quality gate evaluates whether 500 out of 500 passed (green), 498 passed (amber — warn but proceed), or 470 passed (red — block). Without a quality gate, the pipeline might continue deploying even if 30 tests failed — because 'the tests ran' is different from 'the tests passed at an acceptable level.' In production-grade pipelines, quality gates are typically implemented using tools like <a href='/blog/sonarqube-quality-gate-sdet-interview'>SonarQube's Quality Gate</a> feature, GitHub branch protection rules with required status checks, or custom evaluation scripts that consume test reports and coverage XML.",
