      q: "What's the difference between SAST and DAST in a CI/CD pipeline?",
      a: "SAST (Static Application Security Testing) analyses source code, bytecode, or binaries without executing the application. It looks at the code itself for vulnerability patterns — SQL injection vectors in string concatenation, hard-coded credentials, insecure cryptographic algorithms, missing input validation. SAST runs during the build stage, before the application is packaged. Its advantage is speed and code-level precision; its disadvantage is false positives because it lacks runtime context. DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) tests a running application from the outside — sending malicious inputs, probing for exposed endpoints, testing authentication mechanisms. DAST runs after deployment to a staging or testing environment because it needs a live application. Its advantage is finding runtime-only vulnerabilities like authentication bypass and server misconfiguration; its disadvantage is that it cannot see vulnerabilities that don't manifest at runtime (like hard-coded secrets in unreachable code paths). In a mature DevSecOps pipeline, SAST runs on every commit during the build stage, and DAST runs after every staging deployment. They are complementary, not alternatives — a pipeline with only one is missing an entire category of vulnerabilities."
