      a: "Mass assignment occurs when an API endpoint binds all fields in the incoming JSON body to the backend object without filtering — allowing a user to set fields they should not be able to set, such as role, permissions, is_admin, or account_balance. The test: for every endpoint that accepts a POST, PUT, or PATCH body, send a request containing both valid fields and forbidden fields — e.g., for a profile-update endpoint, send name, email (valid) AND role: 'admin', is_admin: true, permissions: ['*'], account_balance: 999999 (forbidden). Then verify: either the API rejects the request (400 Bad Request — 'unknown field: role'), or the API accepts the request but ignores the forbidden fields (200 OK, then GET the resource and assert the role/permissions/balance are unchanged). The engineering signal: 'I maintain a list of forbidden fields per endpoint — fields that no consumer should ever be able to set. The mass-assignment test sends all forbidden fields with every write request and asserts they are rejected or ignored. Any new field added to the forbidden list is automatically tested on all endpoints on the next CI run.' At Accenture, Mitchell's mass-assignment test caught an endpoint where customers could set their own subscription_tier from 'basic' to 'enterprise' without paying — bypassing the entire payment flow. The test was 15 lines of code and had been missing for 2 years."
