      q: "What is the difference between test maintenance and test creation — and why does it matter for SDET interviews?",
      a: "Test creation is the act of writing a test for the first time — designing the scenario, implementing the page objects, writing the assertions. Test maintenance is the ongoing work of keeping that test reliable as the application, the test data, the environments, and the team change over time. The distinction matters because most SDETs are hired for their test-creation skills (can you build a framework, can you write a Playwright test, can you design a Page Object Model) but are evaluated on their test-maintenance skills (can you keep a 3,000-test suite healthy, can you reduce a 25% flake rate, can you defend the automation budget). The interview question that tests this: 'Walk me through a time you inherited a test suite that was in poor health — what did you do in the first 30 days, and what were the results?' Candidates who only have creation experience describe writing new tests. Candidates with maintenance experience describe triaging failures, quarantining flaky tests, refactoring page objects, and presenting a maintenance metrics dashboard to stakeholders."
