        q: "How do I handle the 'tell me about a mistake you made' question when discussing anti-patterns?",
        a: "<p>Be specific, be honest, and demonstrate what you learned. Avoid the two most common mistakes candidates make: (1) describing a mistake so minor that it reveals you have never made a significant one ('I once used the wrong locator and the test failed — I fixed it immediately'), and (2) describing a mistake with no personal accountability ('the team decided to hard-code test data and it caused problems').</p><p>A strong answer follows this structure: the situation (what you built and why), the outcome (what went wrong, with specifics — dates, numbers, impact), the diagnosis (why it was an anti-pattern, not just bad luck), and the learning (what you do differently now — not just 'I would avoid it' but the specific process, tool, or habit that prevents recurrence). Example: 'At my previous role, I built a test suite where every test used the same admin account. Six months later, the account was decommissioned during an identity-provider migration, and 300 tests failed. The anti-pattern was coupling the entire suite to a single shared dependency. I learned three things: never hard-code credentials, never make every test depend on the same account, and when many tests fail simultaneously with the same error, check shared dependencies before debugging individual tests. I have added a shared-dependency audit to every code review checklist I use since.' This answer works because it is specific, self-aware, and demonstrates that the mistake changed your behaviour — which is what interviewers actually want to see.</p>",
