        q: "How do I do data-driven testing in Robot Framework?",
        a: "<p>Robot Framework's primary data-driven testing pattern is the <strong>Test Template</strong>. Define a keyword that accepts parameters: <code>Login And Verify Role    [Arguments]    \${username}    \${password}    \${expected_message}</code>. Set it as the Test Template under <code>*** Settings ***</code>: <code>Test Template    Login And Verify Role</code>. Each test case under <code>*** Test Cases ***</code> becomes a data row — Robot Framework calls the template keyword with the arguments from each row. The key advantages: (1) each data row appears as a separate test in the HTML report with its own pass/fail status, (2) a failure in one data row does not affect other rows, (3) the data table is human-readable — stakeholders can review test coverage by reading the rows. An alternative pattern uses <code>Gherkin-style</code> Scenario Outlines via the Robot Framework's <code>Template</code> keyword with external data files (CSV, Excel). For loop-based data iteration within a single test case, use the BuiltIn <code>FOR</code> loop: <code>FOR    \${user}    IN    @{USERS}</code> followed by keyword calls and <code>END</code> — but the Test Template pattern is preferred for granular reporting.</p>"
