        a: "<p>The standard integration uses the Robot Framework Jenkins plugin. The Jenkins job executes Robot Framework as a shell command: <code>robot --outputdir results/ tests/</code>. The post-build action 'Publish Robot Framework test results' points to <code>results/output.xml</code> — the plugin parses the XML output and generates trend graphs, tag-based filtering, and suite-level drill-down reports in Jenkins. For production pipelines, the advanced pattern: (1) Use the <code>--rerunfailed</code> option to re-execute only failing tests — <code>robot --rerunfailed results/output.xml --outputdir rerun/ tests/</code> — and merge results with <code>rebot --merge results/output.xml rerun/output.xml</code>. (2) Use <code>--include</code> and <code>--exclude</code> with tags for staged execution — smoke tests on every commit, regression tests nightly. (3) Dockerise the test execution for environment consistency. (4) Use Pabot for parallel execution: <code>pabot --processes 4 tests/</code>. The merged report gives the final pass/fail state, and the trend graphs in Jenkins show test stability over time — more useful than any single run result.</p>"
