        q: "What is the difference between TestNG and JUnit 5?",
        a: "TestNG and JUnit 5 are both Java testing frameworks, but they differ in their design philosophy and key capabilities. TestNG was designed from the start as a testing platform for integration, end-to-end, and data-driven testing at scale — it provides five levels of parallel execution (methods, tests, classes, instances, suites), data providers that can feed test data from external sources (Excel, CSV, JSON, databases), a deep annotation hierarchy with seven lifecycle levels (from BeforeSuite to AfterMethod plus BeforeGroups/AfterGroups), and a listener-based extensibility model. JUnit 5 was designed as a modern unit testing framework that added testing-platform features — it has its own parallel execution (method and class level), parameterised tests, and an extension model. TestNG is stronger for large, data-driven, parallel-executed enterprise test suites where test orchestration is managed by CI engineers via XML configuration. JUnit 5 is stronger for developer-centric teams that want programmatic configuration and cleaner extension APIs. The interview-ready answer acknowledges both frameworks' strengths and describes the scenarios where each is the better choice — rather than declaring one 'better'.",
